1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,018
A branch that the team merged and deleted
a month ago is back on the server.

2
00:00:09,058 --> 00:00:13,937
Nobody intended it. It carries old commits,
it shows up in the branch list, and by

3
00:00:13,977 --> 00:00:17,236
Thursday somebody has built
a new feature on top of it.

4
00:00:17,576 --> 00:00:22,852
No one ran a strange command. One engineer
switched to an old local branch and typed

5
00:00:22,892 --> 00:00:27,914
git push. The line on screen is all Git
said. To understand how an ordinary push

6
00:00:27,954 --> 00:00:32,787
resurrects a deleted branch, you need
to know one thing a fetch never does by

7
00:00:32,827 --> 00:00:37,091
default. Hold on to that zombie
branch. Later you make one yourself.

8
00:00:38,930 --> 00:00:42,473
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

9
00:00:42,513 --> 00:00:46,818
Two subjects today, joined by a common
idea: your clone's picture of other

10
00:00:46,858 --> 00:00:49,931
repositories is a set of
refs that only you maintain.

11
00:00:49,971 --> 00:00:54,570
A remote is your name for another repository,
and a remote-tracking ref such as

12
00:00:54,610 --> 00:00:58,622
origin/main records where one of its
branches was at your last fetch.

13
00:00:58,962 --> 00:01:03,664
First, more than one remote. You build
a fork in plain Git, with origin and

14
00:01:03,704 --> 00:01:08,849
upstream, and set it up so that you fetch
from one repository and push to another.

15
00:01:08,889 --> 00:01:13,149
A fork is a second repository on a
server, made from the shared one.

16
00:01:13,189 --> 00:01:18,396
That setup is called a triangular workflow,
and it is where @{upstream} and @{push}

17
00:01:18,436 --> 00:01:23,644
finally give different answers. You also
meet the small tools around remote URLs: a

18
00:01:23,684 --> 00:01:27,754
separate push URL, URL rewriting,
renaming and removing a remote.

19
00:01:28,094 --> 00:01:33,036
Second, pruning. A fetch creates and moves
remote-tracking refs, but by default it

20
00:01:33,076 --> 00:01:37,775
never deletes one. You will see stale
refs block a fetch, see what gone means,

21
00:01:37,815 --> 00:01:42,636
reproduce the zombie branch from the hook,
and then look at the price of pruning

22
00:01:42,676 --> 00:01:43,487
automatically.

23
00:01:45,327 --> 00:01:46,905
Here's what you'll walk away with.

24
00:01:47,245 --> 00:01:48,657
After this video you can:

25
00:01:48,997 --> 00:01:53,238
Configure origin and upstream for a fork
and say which one each command uses.

26
00:01:53,578 --> 00:01:57,337
Set separate fetch and push URLs,
and rename or remove a remote.

27
00:01:57,677 --> 00:02:00,524
Explain what pruning deletes
and what it never deletes.

28
00:02:00,864 --> 00:02:04,763
Explain how a branch deleted on the
server comes back, and prevent it.

29
00:02:06,603 --> 00:02:11,556
More than one remote. In one sentence: a
repository can have any number of remotes,

30
00:02:11,596 --> 00:02:15,766
each with its own URL, refspecs and
namespace of remote-tracking refs.

31
00:02:15,806 --> 00:02:18,894
Nothing connects them except
the objects they share.

32
00:02:19,234 --> 00:02:24,448
So adding a remote named upstream gives
you refs/remotes/upstream/* next to

33
00:02:24,488 --> 00:02:30,122
refs/remotes/origin/*. Two namespaces. If
both name the same commit, it is stored

34
00:02:30,162 --> 00:02:35,516
once. On screen is today's clone right
after that step: main, origin/main and

35
00:02:35,556 --> 00:02:39,298
upstream/main are three names
for one commit, 510ee94.

36
00:02:39,638 --> 00:02:44,553
By convention origin is the repository
you push to and upstream is the shared

37
00:02:44,593 --> 00:02:48,864
repository you take changes from.
That is a convention about names.

38
00:02:48,904 --> 00:02:51,373
Git attaches no meaning to either word.

39
00:02:51,713 --> 00:02:56,581
A triangular workflow fetches from
one repository and pushes to another.

40
00:02:56,621 --> 00:03:01,625
Which remote a bare git push uses is
decided by settings in a fixed order:

41
00:03:01,665 --> 00:03:07,215
branch.<name>.pushRemote, then remote.pushDefault,
then branch.<name>.remote, then

42
00:03:07,255 --> 00:03:12,873
origin. Which branch name it uses is decided
by push.default, from the video before

43
00:03:12,913 --> 00:03:13,214
last.

44
00:03:13,554 --> 00:03:18,331
Pruning. In one sentence: a fetch creates
and moves remote-tracking refs but by

45
00:03:18,371 --> 00:03:23,331
default never deletes one, so branches
deleted on the server live on in your clone

46
00:03:23,371 --> 00:03:24,307
until you prune.

47
00:03:24,647 --> 00:03:28,904
Three commands. git remote prune
--dry-run <remote> previews.

48
00:03:28,944 --> 00:03:34,821
git fetch --prune 🟡 CAUTION deletes
remote-tracking refs that no longer exist on the

49
00:03:34,861 --> 00:03:39,753
server and then fetches. And
fetch.prune=true makes every fetch prune.

50
00:03:40,093 --> 00:03:45,277
Quick quiz. A branch was deleted on the
server, and you run git fetch --prune.

51
00:03:45,317 --> 00:03:49,764
What does it delete in your clone?
A, the remote-tracking ref only.

52
00:03:49,804 --> 00:03:53,917
B, the remote-tracking ref and
your local branch. Your answer?

53
00:03:58,757 --> 00:04:03,125
It's A. What pruning deletes: stale
remote-tracking refs and their reflogs.

54
00:04:03,165 --> 00:04:07,063
What it never touches: local branches,
and their upstream settings.

55
00:04:07,103 --> 00:04:11,178
A local branch whose upstream ref
has been pruned is reported as gone.

56
00:04:11,518 --> 00:04:16,059
When not to prune automatically? Section
12.16 has the sentence: pruning is

57
00:04:16,099 --> 00:04:20,945
forgetting. A remote-tracking ref may be
the last name that some commits have in

58
00:04:20,985 --> 00:04:26,076
your clone. Prune it and its reflog goes
with it. If your clones are the team's only

59
00:04:26,116 --> 00:04:27,969
safety net, prune deliberately.

60
00:04:29,809 --> 00:04:34,695
A picture helps. Go back to the address
book from the first video of this module.

61
00:04:34,735 --> 00:04:37,858
Each remote is one entry
with its own filing drawer.

62
00:04:37,898 --> 00:04:42,845
Two remotes, two drawers. A fetch files
fresh copies into a drawer and updates the

63
00:04:42,885 --> 00:04:47,772
ones already there. It does not go through
the drawer and throw out the bookmarks

64
00:04:47,812 --> 00:04:51,908
for things the supplier has
discontinued. Pruning is that clean-out.

65
00:04:52,248 --> 00:04:56,640
Where the picture breaks: in an office,
an out-of-date bookmark is clutter.

66
00:04:56,680 --> 00:05:01,486
In Git it can be two things at once. It
can be a trap, because a local branch that

67
00:05:01,526 --> 00:05:05,446
still points at a discontinued name
can re-create it with one push.

68
00:05:05,486 --> 00:05:10,351
And it can be a rescue, because that stale
bookmark may be the only name your clone

69
00:05:10,391 --> 00:05:14,902
has for commits that somebody deleted
by mistake. The clean-out removes both.

70
00:05:16,742 --> 00:05:22,276
Try it now. Thirty seconds, on paper. Draw
three boxes: the shared repository, your

71
00:05:22,316 --> 00:05:27,850
clone, and your fork. Add one arrow for
fetch and one for push, and write origin or

72
00:05:27,890 --> 00:05:33,088
upstream next to the right box. Then say
out loud which box your pushes go to.

73
00:05:37,928 --> 00:05:42,733
Here's the answer. Changes come in from
the top and go out at the bottom, to your

74
00:05:42,773 --> 00:05:47,637
fork, origin. The pull request, which is
a GitHub object and not a Git one, closes

75
00:05:47,677 --> 00:05:50,567
the triangle from the
bottom box back to the top.

76
00:05:52,407 --> 00:05:54,538
Part 1: a fork, in plain Git.

77
00:05:54,878 --> 00:05:58,339
A server-side bare clone
stands in for the Fork button.

78
00:05:58,379 --> 00:06:03,304
You clone your fork, add upstream, fetch
it. git remote -v now has four lines.

79
00:06:03,644 --> 00:06:09,083
Two namespaces, origin/* and upstream/*,
each with its own HEAD, all naming the same

80
00:06:09,123 --> 00:06:09,540
commit.

81
00:06:09,880 --> 00:06:15,201
Git has no notion of a fork. The Fork button
creates a GitHub object: a server-side

82
00:06:15,241 --> 00:06:20,563
copy that stays connected to its parent and
shares Git data with it in a repository

83
00:06:20,603 --> 00:06:23,986
network. To Git it is one
more repository with a URL.

84
00:06:24,026 --> 00:06:29,348
According to the documentation, gh repo
fork --clone names your fork origin and the

85
00:06:29,388 --> 00:06:32,061
parent upstream, the convention used here.

86
00:06:32,401 --> 00:06:33,811
Part 2: the triangle.

87
00:06:34,151 --> 00:06:39,139
Predict: after one commit and a bare git
push 🟡, which repository receives the

88
00:06:39,179 --> 00:06:42,684
branch? And what do @{upstream}
and @{push} resolve to?

89
00:06:42,724 --> 00:06:44,425
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

90
00:06:49,265 --> 00:06:50,592
The push went to the fork.

91
00:06:50,932 --> 00:06:55,385
@{upstream} is upstream/main, and
@{push} is origin/feature/streaming.

92
00:06:55,425 --> 00:07:00,584
Two different refs. remote.pushDefault=origin
sends every bare push to your fork,

93
00:07:00,624 --> 00:07:05,911
whatever the upstream of the branch is, and
the branch tracks upstream/main, so git

94
00:07:05,951 --> 00:07:08,863
pull takes changes from
the shared repository.

95
00:07:09,203 --> 00:07:14,789
labs/run ch12/push-destination
takes the settings one at a time.

96
00:07:15,129 --> 00:07:21,342
With only remote.pushDefault set, the push
goes to the fork under the branch's own

97
00:07:21,382 --> 00:07:26,452
name, but Git 2.55 cannot answer
@{push} under push.default=simple.

98
00:07:26,792 --> 00:07:31,687
push.default=current makes the destination
a plain function of the branch name.

99
00:07:31,727 --> 00:07:36,935
The for-each-ref line is the one to remember
in an incident: for every branch, where

100
00:07:36,975 --> 00:07:39,246
it pulls from and where it pushes to.

101
00:07:39,586 --> 00:07:43,734
branch.<name>.pushRemote overrides
the rest for one branch.

102
00:07:44,074 --> 00:07:47,298
Back in the fork demo. The
shared repository has moved.

103
00:07:47,638 --> 00:07:53,365
status.compareBranches, Git 2.54 or
later, makes git status report both

104
00:07:53,405 --> 00:07:54,502
relationships.

105
00:07:54,842 --> 00:07:59,850
Your branch has diverged from upstream/main,
which moved to f11a751, and it is up to

106
00:07:59,890 --> 00:08:01,953
date with origin/feature/streaming.

107
00:08:09,493 --> 00:08:14,342
After a rebase onto the moved upstream, the
branch in your fork still holds the old

108
00:08:14,382 --> 00:08:16,816
commit, so the push is not a fast-forward.

109
00:08:17,156 --> 00:08:21,098
git push --force-with-lease 🔴
DANGEROUS, as every forced push.

110
00:08:21,138 --> 00:08:26,300
The five answers are in the previous video.
This is the legitimate case: a branch

111
00:08:26,340 --> 00:08:28,612
that only you push to, with a lease.

112
00:08:35,952 --> 00:08:41,004
Bringing the fork's main up to date is a
fast-forward from upstream/main and a push

113
00:08:41,044 --> 00:08:41,618
to origin.

114
00:08:41,958 --> 00:08:46,906
Part 3: URL tools.
labs/run ch12/remote-urls.

115
00:08:47,246 --> 00:08:51,292
git remote set-url --push 🟡 gives
a remote a separate push URL.

116
00:08:51,332 --> 00:08:56,285
Here it is deliberately not a repository:
a cheap guard that makes pushing to

117
00:08:56,325 --> 00:09:01,733
upstream impossible from this clone. The
manual warns that a real fetch URL and push

118
00:09:01,773 --> 00:09:05,040
URL of one remote must lead
to the same repository.

119
00:09:05,080 --> 00:09:07,764
For two different places, use two remotes.

120
00:09:08,104 --> 00:09:13,353
Several push URLs make one push update
several repositories, one after the other and

121
00:09:13,393 --> 00:09:14,298
not atomically.

122
00:09:14,638 --> 00:09:17,938
url.<base>.insteadOf rewrites
URLs when they are used.

123
00:09:17,978 --> 00:09:23,072
The configuration keeps the URL that was
written, and git remote get-url shows what

124
00:09:23,112 --> 00:09:24,185
will be contacted.

125
00:09:31,325 --> 00:09:36,104
git remote rename 🟡 moves the remote-tracking
refs and rewrites the configuration,

126
00:09:36,144 --> 00:09:41,041
including the upstream of main. git remote
remove 🟡 deletes the remote-tracking refs

127
00:09:41,081 --> 00:09:45,978
with their reflogs and removes upstream
settings that pointed at the remote: main is

128
00:09:46,018 --> 00:09:46,859
left with none.

129
00:09:47,199 --> 00:09:52,705
Part 4: stale refs. labs/run
ch12/prune-gone. This runs in Asha's clone.

130
00:09:52,745 --> 00:09:59,021
Since she last fetched, feature/reranker
was merged and deleted on the server, and

131
00:09:59,061 --> 00:10:03,026
the team replaced the branch
release by release/1.0.

132
00:10:03,366 --> 00:10:08,862
git branch -r reads the stale local view:
it still lists origin/feature/reranker and

133
00:10:08,902 --> 00:10:11,960
origin/release. git
ls-remote reads the server.

134
00:10:12,300 --> 00:10:17,923
git remote show origin contacts the server
and classifies every branch as tracked,

135
00:10:17,963 --> 00:10:21,031
stale or new. Now an
ordinary fetch. Predict.

136
00:10:25,871 --> 00:10:31,373
Exit status 1. A stale ref can block a
fetch. origin/release still exists, so

137
00:10:31,413 --> 00:10:37,419
origin/release/1.0 cannot be created: one
ref name cannot be a directory-like prefix

138
00:10:37,459 --> 00:10:40,154
of another, in any ref storage format.

139
00:10:40,494 --> 00:10:46,164
The dry run previews. git fetch --prune
deletes the two stale refs and then creates

140
00:10:46,204 --> 00:10:46,990
the new one.

141
00:10:47,330 --> 00:10:52,682
Pruning never touches local branches.
feature/reranker is still there, and its

142
00:10:52,722 --> 00:10:58,420
upstream is now reported as gone.
%(upstream:track) gives the same fact to scripts.

143
00:10:58,760 --> 00:11:04,024
Part 5: the zombie. Asha switches to that
branch. Predict what git pull does and

144
00:11:04,064 --> 00:11:05,283
what git push does.

145
00:11:10,123 --> 00:11:14,866
git pull fails with a clear message. git
push succeeds and re-creates the branch on

146
00:11:14,906 --> 00:11:19,476
the server. That is the hook: a branch
that the team merged and deleted is back,

147
00:11:19,516 --> 00:11:23,049
with old commits. One ordinary
push, and nothing looked wrong.

148
00:11:23,389 --> 00:11:27,366
Delete it on the server again,
🔴 git push origin --delete.

149
00:11:27,406 --> 00:11:32,907
Bring main up to date. And delete the
local branch with -d, which refuses if the

150
00:11:32,947 --> 00:11:34,499
commits are not merged.

151
00:11:34,839 --> 00:11:38,084
With fetch.prune=true every fetch prunes.

152
00:11:38,424 --> 00:11:45,257
Part 6: the price. labs/run ch12/prune-forgets.
Asha has deleted spike/hybrid-search

153
00:11:45,297 --> 00:11:49,184
on the server. Your clone
has not fetched since.

154
00:11:49,524 --> 00:11:53,678
Before the prune, your clone still
named the two commits, and only the

155
00:11:53,718 --> 00:11:58,590
remote-tracking ref contained them. Afterwards
the ref is gone, its reflog is gone

156
00:11:58,630 --> 00:12:01,885
with it, and git fsck lists
the commits as unreachable.

157
00:12:01,925 --> 00:12:06,857
They stay in the object database until
garbage collection, Git's cleanup of objects

158
00:12:06,897 --> 00:12:08,954
that nothing reaches, removes them.

159
00:12:09,294 --> 00:12:15,820
Until then git fsck --lost-found can find
the tip, 509f067, and a branch gives it a

160
00:12:15,860 --> 00:12:22,386
name again. With fetch.prune=true this
forgetting happens on every fetch, without a

161
00:12:22,426 --> 00:12:23,019
preview.

162
00:12:24,859 --> 00:12:26,507
Five mistakes to watch for.

163
00:12:26,847 --> 00:12:30,042
Pushing from an old local
branch whose upstream is gone.

164
00:12:30,082 --> 00:12:34,778
Root cause: pruning removes the remote-tracking
ref but keeps the local branch and

165
00:12:34,818 --> 00:12:38,879
its upstream setting, so a push creates
the branch on the server again.

166
00:12:39,219 --> 00:12:42,356
Believing git branch -r
lists the server's branches.

167
00:12:42,396 --> 00:12:47,490
Root cause: it lists remote-tracking refs
in your clone, which a default fetch never

168
00:12:47,530 --> 00:12:47,979
deletes.

169
00:12:48,319 --> 00:12:52,450
Pushing a topic branch to the
shared repository in a fork setup.

170
00:12:52,490 --> 00:12:57,926
Root cause: without remote.pushDefault or
branch.<name>.pushRemote, a branch created

171
00:12:57,966 --> 00:13:00,924
from upstream/main has
upstream as its remote.

172
00:13:01,264 --> 00:13:06,018
Setting fetch.prune=true everywhere and
counting on clones as a safety net.

173
00:13:06,058 --> 00:13:11,195
Root cause: pruning deletes the ref and
its reflog, which may be the last name of

174
00:13:11,235 --> 00:13:13,559
commits that were deleted by mistake.

175
00:13:13,899 --> 00:13:18,522
Giving one remote a fetch URL and a push
URL that are different repositories.

176
00:13:18,562 --> 00:13:23,608
Root cause: the manual requires both to lead
to the same repository; two places need

177
00:13:23,648 --> 00:13:24,335
two remotes.

178
00:13:26,175 --> 00:13:31,040
Now, out of the lab. You contribute a fix
to an open-source evaluation library.

179
00:13:31,080 --> 00:13:36,194
Fork, clone, add upstream, branch from
upstream/main, push to origin, open the pull

180
00:13:36,234 --> 00:13:41,410
request. Inside a company the same triangle
appears when a team keeps a patched copy

181
00:13:41,450 --> 00:13:46,501
of a vendor repository. The textbook's
advice for both: write down which remote is

182
00:13:46,541 --> 00:13:48,240
which before the first push.

183
00:13:48,580 --> 00:13:53,977
On the pruning side, picture a platform
team that sets fetch.prune=true in every

184
00:13:54,017 --> 00:13:58,054
developer's global configuration
to keep branch lists short.

185
00:13:58,094 --> 00:13:59,753
It works well for months.

186
00:14:00,093 --> 00:14:04,737
Then someone deletes the wrong branch on
the server, a spike that was never merged.

187
00:14:04,777 --> 00:14:09,194
Within a day every clone that fetches
forgets it. That is acceptable for merged

188
00:14:09,234 --> 00:14:11,677
feature branches and
costly on that one day.

189
00:14:12,017 --> 00:14:17,663
Section 12.16 lists more cases where a
technique from this module is the wrong one.

190
00:14:17,703 --> 00:14:23,349
Three to remember. git push --mirror and
git push --prune delete refs on the server

191
00:14:23,389 --> 00:14:28,624
that your repository lacks. They are
migration tools for a mirror clone and 🔴

192
00:14:28,664 --> 00:14:29,720
everywhere else.

193
00:14:30,060 --> 00:14:35,773
A token inside the remote URL stores the
secret in plain text in .git/config, and

194
00:14:35,813 --> 00:14:39,964
git remote -v prints it wherever
it runs, CI logs included.

195
00:14:40,004 --> 00:14:45,789
Use a credential helper. And a forced push
is not a way to remove a leaked secret.

196
00:14:45,829 --> 00:14:47,494
Rotate the secret first.

197
